# Sports Tournament & Clothing Store Database System

## Overview

This project is a relational database system designed for managing sports tournaments, teams, matches, and clothing store employees.

The system combines sports tournament management with a clothing store management concept, where players, coaches, and referees are also employees of a clothing store.

The project was designed using:

* ERD (Entity Relationship Diagram)
* Relational Schema (DSD)
* SQL queries
* PostgreSQL / MySQL compatible schema

---

# System Description

The database manages:

* National teams
* Players
* Coaches
* Referees
* Matches
* Stadiums
* Match events
* Player statistics
* Tournaments
* Clothing stores

In the updated version of the project:

* Teams participate in matches
* Clothing stores organize tournaments
* Players, coaches, and referees work for clothing stores

---

# ERD (Entity Relationship Diagram)

## Main Entities

* NationalTeam
* Player
* Coach
* Referee
* Match
* MatchEvent
* Stadium
* Tournament
* ClothingStore
* PlayerStatistics

---

# Main Relationships

## Team Relationships

* A national team has many players (1:N)
* A national team has one coach (1:1)
* National teams participate in matches (N:N)

## Match Relationships

* A match includes many match events (1:N)
* A match contains many player statistics (1:N)
* A match is officiated by one referee (N:1)
* A match is played in one stadium (N:1)
* A tournament includes many matches (1:N)

## Clothing Store Relationships

* A clothing store organizes tournaments (1:N)
* A clothing store employs players (1:N)
* A clothing store employs coaches (1:N)
* A clothing store employs referees (1:N)

---

# DSD (Database Schema Design)

## NationalTeam

* team_id (PK)
* team_name
* country
* team_rank
* team_colors
* founded_date
* sport_type
* team_details_json

---

## Player

* player_id (PK)
* first_name
* last_name
* birth_date
* nationality
* position
* height
* jersey_number
* extra_info_json
* team_id (FK)
* store_id (FK)

---

## Coach

* coach_id (PK)
* first_name
* last_name
* birth_date
* nationality
* years_of_experience
* contract_start_date
* team_id (FK)
* store_id (FK)

---

## Referee

* referee_id (PK)
* first_name
* last_name
* birth_date
* nationality
* certification_level
* years_of_experience
* store_id (FK)

---

## Stadium

* stadium_id (PK)
* stadium_name
* city
* country
* capacity
* build_date
* stadium_type

---

## Match

* match_id (PK)
* match_date
* stage
* status
* home_score
* away_score
* attendance
* weather_json
* stadium_id (FK)
* referee_id (FK)

---

## MatchEvent

* event_id (PK)
* event_type
* event_minute
* event_description
* severity_level
* event_data_json
* match_id (FK)

---

## PlayerStatistics

* stat_id (PK)
* stat_date
* minutes_played
* points_or_goals
* assists
* fouls
* yellow_cards
* red_cards
* player_id (FK)
* match_id (FK)

---

## Tournament

* tournament_id (PK)
* tournament_name
* season
* start_date
* end_date
* location
* store_id (FK)

---

## ClothingStore

* store_id (PK)
* store_name
* brand_name
* website
* city
* phone

---

# Design Decisions

* Numeric primary keys are used for simplicity and performance.
* Foreign keys maintain relationships and data integrity.
* JSON fields are used for flexible and dynamic information.
* The system supports both tournament management and employee management.
* Many-to-many relationships are used where teams can participate in multiple matches.

---

# Assumptions

* Each player belongs to one national team.
* Each team has one coach.
* Each match has one referee.
* Each match takes place in one stadium.
* Each tournament is organized by one clothing store.
* Players, coaches, and referees work for clothing stores.

---

